            %em Bishop set
            is a notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop%27s+constructive+mathematics"} Bishop's constructive mathematics
            (cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+analysis"} constructive analysis
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Bishop"}> Bishop
            ) the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is specified by stating that a set has to be given by a description of how to build
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            of this set and by giving a binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is then given by an operation, which is compatible with the equality (i.e. two elements which are equal in A are mapped to two elements which are equal in B), and is described as “a finite routine
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            which assigns an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to each given element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            to the procedure for constructing
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %p
            These ideas have been formalized in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            in an ambient type theoretic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+framework"} logical framework
            %em
              %a{:href => "#FormalizationInTypeTheory"} Formalization in type theory
            below.
          %h2#FormalizationInTypeTheory Formalization in type theory
          %p
            Formally, the definition of Bishop set presumes some prior notion of “collection” (the elements) and “relation” (the equality) that can be put together. These collections are sometimes called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presets"} presets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            and even
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
          %p
            One of the most common approaches is to use
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            — in the latter, type theory is enriched with operations on types such as quotients, enabling them to be the basic set-like objects out of which mathematics is built; while in the Bishop-set approach one defines “sets” as types equipped with extra structure, so that the sets have quotient-type operations even though the types do not. There is an analogy to the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+completion"} exact completion
            of a category, which has quotients even if the original category does not.
          %p
            Frequently, the type theories used for this purpose do not even have a separate notion of “relation” or “proposition”, and thus the untruncated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            interpretation is used for the equivalence relations in a Bishop set. That is, one has a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X:Type
            of elements and a type family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R:X\to X \to Type
            with witnesses that it is “reflexive, symmetric, and transitive”, but nothing prevents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(x,y)
            from having more than one element. (If the type theory has
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            consist of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-propositions"} h-propositions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ex%2Flex+completion"} ex/lex completion
            of the category of types — although the morphisms in the ex/lex completion are defined as a
            %em quotient
            in the ambient set theory, whereas the morphisms between Bishop sets simply form another Bishop set.
          %p
            For example, see (
            %a{:href => "#Palmgren05"}> Palmgren05
            ) for an exposition and (
            %a{:href => "#CoquandSpiwack"}> Coquand-Spiwack, section 3
            ) for a brief list of the axioms.
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            Bishop sets are also called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoids"} setoids
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#the_predicative_topos_of_bishop_sets The predicative topos of Bishop sets
          %p
            In much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of all sets is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            this is not quite the case. Instead:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The category of Bishop sets in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+predicative+topos"} strong predicative topos
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#vdBerg"}> van den Berg, theorem 6.2
            ), based on (
            %a{:href => "#MoerdijkPalmgren"}> Moerdijk-Palmgren, section 7
            ).
